`chmod` changes the permissions of files and directories, controlling what the owner, group, and others can access.
`ls -l` displays detailed information about files, including permissions, ownership, size, and timestamps.
`useradd` creates a new Linux user account and can configure its home directory and login shell.
In this task, these commands demonstrate user management, file permissions, and secure access control.
Together, they help organize and manage a Linux workspace securely.
